                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Stainless Humidifiers Actually on the Market
          </h2>

          <p>
            The stainless humidifier category is small and dominated by one brand, with a scattering of smaller names selling through marketplaces. Before buying any of them, check the listing for exactly which parts are steel, because the word stainless is sometimes attached to a model where only a lid trim or an internal plate is metal. The specifications below are the manufacturers' own figures, not measurements of ours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carepod: the brand that built the category</h3>

          <p>
            Carepod is the name most people mean when they search for a stainless steel humidifier. Its main models pair a stainless steel water tank with a filterless ultrasonic design built to come apart into a few pieces for cleaning, and Carepod markets its stainless tanks as dishwasher safe. The Carepod One is the core single-room model, which Carepod lists with a tank of about 4 liters, coverage of up to roughly 500 square feet and a run time of around 30 hours on low. The Cube line is the step-up, adding warm mist alongside cool mist and a heat-based sanitizing feature, with Carepod listing a slightly larger tank and coverage of up to about 700 square feet for the Cube Plus.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two cautions apply. Some independent reviewers have reported a usable tank volume noticeably lower than the advertised capacity, so treat the run-time claims as best case and expect to refill more often than the box suggests. And Carepod's own comparison material indicates that not every Carepod model uses a stainless tank, with the smaller Mini listed differently from the One and Cube, so confirm the tank material on the specific model rather than assuming it from the brand name.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smaller stainless brands</h3>

          <p>
            Beyond Carepod, several smaller brands sell ultrasonic humidifiers with 304 stainless tanks through online marketplaces, typically in the 3.5 to 4.5 liter range, often described as top-fill and dishwasher safe. Specifications for these vary between listings and retailers, sometimes for what appears to be the same unit, and reviews are mostly short-term. They can be a reasonable way to get a steel tank for less, but buy from a seller with a clear return policy, read the manual before you trust the dishwasher-safe label, and expect the same plastic base and transducer as everything else in the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what you want is easy cleaning rather than steel specifically, it is worth widening the search. Several mainstream plastic humidifiers now use wide-mouth, top-fill tanks with smooth interiors you can reach with a hand or sponge, and some evaporative models have dishwasher-safe parts. Our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier picks</a> cover those designs alongside the stainless options, and the <a href="/types-of-humidifiers/">types of humidifiers guide</a> explains which design suits which room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Price, Weight and the Practical Trade-Offs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most obvious trade-off is cost. Stainless steel humidifiers typically sell for two to four times the price of a plastic ultrasonic humidifier with a similar tank size and coverage rating. Part of that is material and manufacturing, and part of it is that these are positioned as premium products with a cleaner design and a quieter, more furniture-like look. You are paying for the steel, the cleaning-friendly design and the styling together, so it is worth deciding which of those you actually value before comparing prices. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-humidifier-cost/">how much a humidifier costs</a> sets out the full range by type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running cost is close to identical. A filterless ultrasonic humidifier uses little electricity, typically in the range of a few tens of watts in cool mist mode, regardless of what the tank is made from, and there are no wicks to replace. The ongoing expense that does matter is water. If your tap water is hard, you will either buy distilled water through the winter or live with white dust, and that cost is the same for steel and plastic. Models with a warm mist mode use considerably more power when the heater is on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is a smaller issue than it sounds. A full 4 liter tank holds about 4 kilograms, or close to 9 pounds, of water, and that dominates what you carry to the sink. The steel shell adds a little on top, and the tank is less forgiving of a knock, since a dent in a metal tank is permanent and can affect how it seats on the base. Carry it with two hands, set it down gently, and do not drop it into a hard sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Durability is where steel earns some of its premium back. A plastic tank that has been scrubbed weekly for three winters is often cloudy, scratched and harder to get truly clean, and some owners replace the whole humidifier at that point. A steel tank should look much the same after years of the same treatment. The electronics in the base are the likelier failure point on any ultrasonic humidifier, so the warranty on the base matters as much as the tank, and it is worth reading before you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
